  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\nThe upper level disturbance that is producing all the rain/snow\nthis morning continues to move eastward, and should exit the\neastern portions of the CWA this afternoon. Thus morning showers\nare likely, especially over North Central MT, with showers ending\nthis afternoon for most areas. \n\nFor the next several days, the flow aloft will shift to a more\nwesterly flow. This is a bit of a change from previous forecasts.\nThus with a more westerly flow, the chances for precipitation will\nbe confined mainly to our western mountains, with just a small\nchance for an isolated shower east of the divide.\n\nThe next more widespread weather system looks to arrive Sun into\nMon, as a fairly strong upper level low moves from WA\nsoutheastward towards UT. This will result in a period of\nrain/snow showers moving through our CWA as the disturbance passes\nto our south.\n\nWith the westerly flow aloft, afternoon temperatures will be close\nto seasonal normals, or just a touch below for most areas over the\nnext few days.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\nSnow will fall over the Rocky Mountain Front today, mostly for\nelevations above 5000 feet. The best accumulating snow will fall\nover the northern Rockies with a few inches expected today. Since\nLogan's Pass is closed, no advisory will be issued at this time.\nShould impacts start to develop on Marias Pass, a winter weather\nadvisory might be needed for a short time.\n\nFor this weekend, snow amounts of 3 to 6 inches are in the current\nforecast for the mountains around Big Sky, Bozeman and northward\nto Kings Hill. Thus a winter statement might be needed this\nweekend for some mountain ranges.\n\nLooking ahead to the middle of next week, so forecast models are\nshowing more of an upper level ridge, rather than a westerly flow\naloft. Should the upper level ridge pan out, afternoon\ntemperatures could be a few degrees warmer than currently\nforecast. Brusda",
